.Dd October 27, 2026
.Dt XFT_FILES 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_open_file ,
.Nm xft_close_file ,
.Nm xft_file_info
.Nd open, close and describe files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In files.h
.Ft t_fd
.Fn xft_open_file "t_blk8r path" "t_fileopen_mode mode"
.Ft t_fd
.Fn xft_close_file "t_fd fd"
.Ft t_file_info
.Fn xft_file_info "t_blk8r path"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The functions work the same way on Linux and Windows: Linux uses
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr close 2
and
.Xr statx 2 ,
and Windows calls
.Ql CreateFileA ,
.Ql CloseHandle
and
.Ql GetFileInformationByHandleEx
directly.
Paths are bytes passed through unchanged; on Windows they go to the ANSI API.
.Ss Types
.Vt t_fd
is a
.Vt t_u32a
descriptor on Linux and on 32-bit Windows, and a
.Vt t_uptr
holding a
.Vt HANDLE
on 64-bit Windows.
.Pp
.Vt t_fileopen_mode
is a bit-field of
.Fa read ,
.Fa write ,
.Fa truncate ,
.Fa append ,
.Fa create
and
.Fa exclusive .
.Pp
.Vt t_file_info
has the members
.Fa size ,
.Fa blk_size ,
.Fa n_blks ,
.Fa acc_time ,
.Fa mod_time ,
.Fa chg_time ,
.Fa bth_time ,
.Fa inode ,
.Fa dev_id ,
.Fa lnk_cnt ,
.Fa perms
and
.Fa type .
The four times are nanoseconds since the Unix epoch.
.Fa perms
is a
.Vt t_file_perms
bit-field laid out like the low 12 bits of
.Fa st_mode .
.Fa type
is one of
.Dv F_T_SYML ,
.Dv F_T_DISK ,
.Dv F_T_SOCK ,
.Dv F_T_BLK ,
.Dv F_T_DEV ,
.Dv F_T_PIPE ,
.Dv F_T_DIR
or
.Dv F_T_UNKNOWN .
.Ss Opening
.Fn xft_open_file
opens
.Fa path .
.Fa append
implies
.Fa write ,
and
.Fa exclusive
implies
.Fa create
and fails if the file exists.
New files get mode 0666, reduced by the umask on Linux.
On Windows the file is shared for reading, writing and deletion, and
.Fa append
opens it without
.Dv FILE_WRITE_DATA ,
so every write lands at the end of the file as with
.Dv O_APPEND .
.Pp
.Fn xft_close_file
closes
.Fa fd .
.Ss Describing
.Fn xft_file_info
describes
.Fa path
without following a final symbolic link, like
.Xr lstat 2 .
On Linux it asks
.Xr statx 2
for the basic fields and the birth time;
.Fa bth_time
is 0 on file systems that do not record it, and
.Fa dev_id
is
.Ql major << 32 | minor .
.Pp
On Windows:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Fa type
is
.Dv F_T_SYML
for name-surrogate reparse points (symbolic links and junctions),
.Dv F_T_SOCK
for AF_UNIX sockets,
.Dv F_T_DIR
for directories, and otherwise follows
.Ql GetFileType .
.It
there are no mode bits, so
.Fa perms
grants read to everyone, write unless the file is read-only, and execute on
directories.
.It
.Fa n_blks
counts 512-byte blocks of the allocation size, like
.Fa st_blocks ,
and
.Fa blk_size
is the volume's preferred I/O size.
.It
.Fa inode
is the file index and
.Fa dev_id
the volume serial number.
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_open_file
returns the new descriptor, and
.Fn xft_close_file
returns 0.
Both return
.Ql (t_fd)-1
on failure, which on Windows is
.Dv INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE .
.Pp
.Fn xft_file_info
returns the description, or a zeroed one whose
.Fa type
is
.Dv F_T_UNKNOWN
on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Create a file only if it does not exist:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

t_result	create_new(const char *path)
{
	t_fd	fd;

	fd = xft_open_file((t_blk8r)path,
			(t_fileopen_mode){.write = 1, .exclusive = 1});
	if (fd == (t_fd)-1)
		return (KO);
	return (xft_close_file(fd) == 0);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_io 3 ,
.Xr xft_os 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
.In files.h
also declares
.Vt t_fs_opts ,
.Vt t_file_lock
and
.Vt t_seek ,
which no function takes.
.Pp
.Fn xft_file_info
cannot tell a failure apart from a real file of type
.Dv F_T_UNKNOWN .
.Pp
On Windows,
.Fa append
together with
.Fa truncate
may fail, because truncation needs
.Dv FILE_WRITE_DATA .
.Pp
On Windows a non-ASCII path only opens when the ANSI code page is UTF-8.
